WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.850
- So where are we going now?

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.191
- We are going to our--

00:00:05.191 --> 00:00:06.424
- The cabin.

00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:07.540
- The cabin?

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.460
(electric powwow dance music)

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.220
- This place is, we fell in love with it

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.720
when we started working here,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.530
it's like this little
hidden spot in the city.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.330
- [Nahre] Electronic dance
music has always been

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.943
fertile ground for the
blending of musical genres.

00:00:20.943 --> 00:00:24.050
Canadian DJs 2oolMan and Bear Witness,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:27.000
collectively known as A Tribe Called Red,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.046
combine the sounds of traditional
native powwow drum circles

00:00:30.046 --> 00:00:33.260
with electronic dance music
to create something they call

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.283
electric powwow.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.160
- We're here.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.130
- [Nahre] I was able to sit
in with A Tribe Called Red

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:41.356
as they started to work
on a new batch of music.

00:00:41.356 --> 00:00:42.189
- [2oolman] So it's a little
bit of a tight squeeze

00:00:42.189 --> 00:00:43.114
getting in here

00:00:43.114 --> 00:00:44.140
(laughs)

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:45.580
- [Nahre] At a hard-to-find studio

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.621
on a residential street in Toronto.

00:00:47.621 --> 00:00:50.954
(electric powwow music)

00:00:56.704 --> 00:00:58.183
- [2oolman] I love this unit.

00:00:58.183 --> 00:01:00.693
- [Nahre] So how did
Tribe Called Red start?

00:01:01.548 --> 00:01:03.120
- It all grew out of a club night.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.030
- [Nahre] A club night?

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.290
- Yeah, so back in like 2008,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:08.820
a few of us got together in Ottawa.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.430
We didn't really know
each other all that well

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:12.990
but just came together under the idea

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.500
that we're all Native
DJs working in the city

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.610
and let's throw a party together.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.640
The reaction that we got
from our community was that,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.980
you've started something, you've
created this space for us,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.480
and you have to keep doing it now.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.196
- [Nahre] Did you feel
pressure in any way?

00:01:28.196 --> 00:01:29.450
- Not so much pre...

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:31.790
Well, I mean there was a
really quick realization

00:01:31.790 --> 00:01:32.840
that we were doing something

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.650
much bigger than we thought we were.

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.210
We thought we were just throwing a party

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.050
and would advertise to our community,

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.570
but really quickly we realized that

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:42.830
it meant a lot more to them than that.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.980
That taking up space within
the urban environment

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.810
as indigenous people is a
difficult thing sometimes.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.530
To actually have a community and a culture

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.083
that is urban and is
indigenous, isn't new.

00:01:55.083 --> 00:02:00.083
We've been here since these
were forts and villages,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.722
but our visibility has changed.

00:02:03.722 --> 00:02:04.880
- [Nahre] You guys are pioneering then.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:06.530
- Well, I mean--
- [Nahre] Parts of it?

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.180
- I've always said that
we're part of a wave,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.170
like we threw this party and got told

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:13.996
we had to keep doing it
by people in the community

00:02:13.996 --> 00:02:17.761
so we're always following that
wave, not really leading it.

00:02:17.761 --> 00:02:21.094
(electric powwow music)

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.380
- [Nahre] When you're
creating new projects

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.210
do you keep in mind
this cultural landscape,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.800
this wave that you're talking about?

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.800
- I mean, I guess it's finding a balance

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.240
between those things.

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.464
Break it down into DJ terms,
you're reading the room.

00:02:39.464 --> 00:02:42.130
(electric powwow music)

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.220
- [Bear Witness] Our
second album was called

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.150
Nation II Nation because we
were producing it at a time

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.620
when there was a new
movement happening in Canada

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:49.770
called Idle No More.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.700
- A First Nations protest movement

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.900
is quietly spreading across the country.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.410
- First Nations protestors
gathered on Parliament Hill

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.726
once again, and--

00:02:57.726 --> 00:02:58.690
- [Bear Witness]
Indigenous people in Canada

00:02:58.690 --> 00:02:59.747
all together stood up and said,

00:02:59.747 --> 00:03:02.110
"We're not going to quietly
let things happen anymore."

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:05.640
There was this huge influx of
indigenous people in Ottawa

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.340
who came from all over
the country to protest.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.670
So that album, Nation II Nation,

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:11.820
it even got its name, Nation II Nation

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.510
because that's what we're calling for,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:17.850
we're calling to have a
conversation, a talk with Canada,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:21.260
from the indigenous nation
to the settler nation.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.880
So, you know, that vibe,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:24.980
everything that was happening

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.112
got put into the album.

00:03:26.112 --> 00:03:27.470
(electric powwow music)

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:29.070
- [Nahre] The energy of a protest movement

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.470
is only one ingredient in their music.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.260
A Tribe Called Red uses
samples of native drum circles

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.820
as the basis for almost
all of their music.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.660
What started off as an experiment,

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:41.180
sampling drum sounds from
powwow videos they found

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:44.890
on YouTube, has turned
into a collaboration.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.869
- [2oolman] So I was going to
bring this up really quick.

00:03:46.869 --> 00:03:47.702
(computer mouse clicking)

00:03:47.702 --> 00:03:50.960
I asked 'em to give me different patterns,

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.525
so they gave us different
patterns that they sing with.

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:55.358
(powwow drums playing)

00:03:55.358 --> 00:03:58.263
So this is a sidestep
groove, so it's a sidestep.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.852
And then there's straight.

00:04:02.852 --> 00:04:06.340
(computer mouse clicking)
(powwow drums playing)

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.060
- [Bear Witness] The very
first thing we ever did

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:10.890
with powwow music was just
doing a a live mash-up.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.450
And it's because we had a
grass dance song that was open

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.790
at the beginning so you
just had straight singing

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.350
and you could just loop that

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.087
and then threw a dubstep
instrumental underneath it.

00:04:21.087 --> 00:04:23.130
You know, it's like
finding any other sample,

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.327
like finding a breakbeat
that was like a clean break

00:04:25.327 --> 00:04:26.786
that was super funky--

00:04:26.786 --> 00:04:27.619
- [Nahre] Isolated.

00:04:27.619 --> 00:04:28.930
- that you could sample that was isolated.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:32.290
And it's slowly grown into
us having this relationship

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.426
with the drum groups now, where
they give us what we need.

00:04:35.426 --> 00:04:37.770
(powwow drums playing)

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.853
- [2oolman] So it's hitting
off the side of the drum,

00:04:39.853 --> 00:04:41.023
so it's a stick.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.970
- So those drums that you heard,

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:46.920
one of those is in almost every
song that we make (laughs).

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.010
- [Nahre] So what is a drum circle?

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.685
LA went to Prior Lake,
Minnesota to find out.

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:55.140
(powwow music)

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.550
- [LA] I met up with Iron Boy,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.480
an inter-tribal drum circle
based in Minneapolis.

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:01.900
Although they haven't collaborated
with A Tribe Called Red,

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.220
they've been performing at powwows

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:04.450
all over the United States,

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.330
including what's considered the
largest powwow in the world,

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.360
The Gathering of Nations in New Mexico,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.520
where they were crowned champions

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.908
in the Northern Drum Contest in 2015.

00:05:12.908 --> 00:05:16.158
(drumming and singing)

00:05:25.281 --> 00:05:27.770
- I'm breathing fast, like...

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.267
A typical northern drum circle song

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.280
has a call-and-response structure

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.784
divided into pushups, or verses.

00:05:33.784 --> 00:05:37.130
(drumming and singing)

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.530
The first lead will sing a line,

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.630
then the group will respond with a chorus.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:48.840
Once the chorus is sung and repeated,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.800
the next singer in the
circle takes the lead

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.150
to start the second pushup.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.120
Although the sound might feel traditional,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.650
the members of Iron Boy
write their own music,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.200
like this song composed by
member Charles Larrabee.

00:06:08.706 --> 00:06:11.440
- For the most part, I try to visualize

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:15.743
the dancers dancing and just
talk about their movements.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.070
Like (speaking native
language) is talking about

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.420
the women's long braids when they spin

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:23.620
you can see them flying.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.008
- Y'all got chemistry so
learning something new,

00:06:30.008 --> 00:06:32.410
it happens quick, you know what
I'm saying? It seemed like?

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.490
- Well, when there's something
and I feel like it's worthy

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.990
then I'll send it to the boys.

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:40.440
And if it clicks, then we'll
stick with it, you know?

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.494
- [LA] This is very similar
to a jazz band setting

00:06:43.494 --> 00:06:46.350
where we record the
rehearsal, then we send it out

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.648
to all the homies and we
listen to it in the car,

00:06:48.648 --> 00:06:49.481
you know what I'm saying, we're driving

00:06:49.481 --> 00:06:51.570
and then come back knowing your part.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.547
Same, same kind of format.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:06:58.250
- Basically when you have a song,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.134
you kind of have these
basic rules, but you try to

00:07:00.134 --> 00:07:02.530
bend those rules as much as you can.

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:04.190
Like, contemporary music is going to

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:06.000
usually use a lot of words.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.290
Old school is usually
straight songs not many words

00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:13.900
just because of there
was a time up until 1978

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.130
everything was outlawed so really,

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.920
you kind of see this movement now.

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:20.760
A lot of people are moving
back toward some of those ways,

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.870
like using words in a
song and stuff like that,

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.470
a lot of language and taking
pride in that language.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:29.830
- [LA] In 1978, not that
long ago, the US government

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.370
passed the American Indian
Religious Freedom Act,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:33.550
which aimed to protect the rights

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.853
of Native Americans to
practice their culture.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.280
- Something that we value, our culture,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.770
we value it so much that
we're gonna carry this on

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:48.670
until we make our journey
and we're gonna teach

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.400
as many young people as
we can along the way.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.103
It's a huge responsibility.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:01.770
You know, this drum here is
very, very, very, very sacred

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:06.053
to us and this represents
a grandfather spirit.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.603
The drum takes care of us,
we take care of the drum.

00:08:11.611 --> 00:08:14.861
(drumming and singing)

00:08:17.781 --> 00:08:21.114
(electric powwow music)

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:26.970
- [Bear Witness] You know,
instead of using, like,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:30.484
airhorns or sirens like a
lot of electronic music will

00:08:30.484 --> 00:08:33.163
we use the calls, powwow calls.

00:08:33.163 --> 00:08:35.340
(demonstrates powwow calling)

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.516
- [2oolman] Yeah, we got stuff like this.

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:38.035
- Stuff like that that we throw in.

00:08:38.035 --> 00:08:40.571
(powwow call echoes)

00:08:40.571 --> 00:08:42.415
- So that's like our airhorn.
You know what I mean?

00:08:42.415 --> 00:08:44.207
(electronic music)

00:08:44.207 --> 00:08:46.310
- [Bear Witness] But
it has the same effect.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:48.250
You're playing to an indigenous audience,

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.690
those have the same effect.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:52.624
People react to it the same way.

00:08:52.624 --> 00:08:54.810
(imitates air horn blasting)

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:55.930
- It's so crazy.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:08:57.767
I get people who hit me up
all the time and they're like,

00:08:57.767 --> 00:08:59.780
"Hey man, do you think you can

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.847
give me that sound you guys use?

00:09:01.847 --> 00:09:03.427
"'Cause I'd really like to play it

00:09:03.427 --> 00:09:05.497
"at like at a lacrosse game

00:09:05.497 --> 00:09:07.640
"or something like that back home."

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:08.890
So it's like, they want to use that

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:10.767
as a goal horn, you know what I mean!

00:09:10.767 --> 00:09:11.600
(laughing)

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:12.433
It's so weird!

00:09:12.433 --> 00:09:14.170
But that's where we're at right now.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:15.570
- [Bear Witness] Replacing
the airhorns and that,

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.903
that's just a way of indigenizing
the space that we're in.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:23.720
- Yeah, we will add a
ton of other instruments.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.199
Anything we get our hands on, really.

00:09:26.199 --> 00:09:28.026
(electronic effects playing)

00:09:28.026 --> 00:09:29.413
- Like, it goes faster,

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:32.303
slower.

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.000
- Tim made a decision early on
that he wanted all the risers

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.450
to be self-made, so it's a lot of stuff

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.880
that we messed around with in Calgary.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:45.161
- We were in National
Music Centre in Calgary

00:09:45.161 --> 00:09:48.700
and they have just a bunch of
synths, like a synth museum,

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:52.010
but they allow people to
have residencies there.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.540
They'll have access to,

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.760
basically to almost
every single synth known

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.703
even the legendary TONTO.

00:09:57.703 --> 00:09:58.536
(electronic music)

00:09:58.536 --> 00:09:59.810
- [Nahre] This is TONTO,

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.860
or The Original New Timbral Orchestra.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:06.340
It was created by Malcolm
Cecil in the early 1970s.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.150
The room-sized multi-timbral,
polyphonic, analog,

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:11.330
synthesizer was used by many artists

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.290
including the Isley
Brothers and Stevie Wonder.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:17.240
Only one was ever made
and it now lives in Canada

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.293
at the National Music
Centre in Calgary, Alberta.

00:10:22.498 --> 00:10:24.600
(computer mouse clicking)

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:27.081
- I can bring up to OG actually.

00:10:27.081 --> 00:10:29.620
(powwow dance announcer calls)
(powwow drums playing)

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.500
- [Nahre] I asked 2oolman to break down

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:34.400
some of the elements used in
a track they were working on.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.004
- So I'm just playing them through.

00:10:36.004 --> 00:10:39.087
(powwow music plays)

00:10:44.449 --> 00:10:45.282
(computer mouse clicking)

00:10:45.282 --> 00:10:47.130
- [2oolman] I just added
some drums to that.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:50.380
(modified music plays)

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.390
- [2oolman] Yeah, so just added...

00:10:58.434 --> 00:11:01.684
(modified music plays)

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.700
- [Nahre] One more element
for the track was found

00:11:07.700 --> 00:11:10.550
when member of Canadian
Parliament Romeo Saganash

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:13.860
delivered a passionate speech
on behalf of indigenous rights

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:14.801
to the House of Commons.

00:11:14.801 --> 00:11:15.634
(electric powwow music)

00:11:15.634 --> 00:11:17.606
- Why doesn't the Prime
Minister just say the truth

00:11:17.606 --> 00:11:18.707
and tell the indigenous peoples

00:11:18.707 --> 00:11:21.643
that he doesn't give a
(...) about their rights.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:29.650
- Watching indigenous Twitter
and Instagram and Facebook

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:32.860
explode as soon as that
happened was like, okay,

00:11:32.860 --> 00:11:34.750
this is definitely a moment

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:37.035
and I mean we felt that moment ourselves.

00:11:37.035 --> 00:11:37.927
- You become a part of it.

00:11:37.927 --> 00:11:40.790
- Yeah, you wanna do something
with it, because, again,

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.580
like when we first
started making this music

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.440
it was to make something
that everybody could enjoy

00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:45.780
and everyone can appreciate

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.730
but that would be instantly recognizable

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.390
and identifiable to indigenous people.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.250
So to take a moment for indigenous people

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:54.790
and then weave it into a song, you know,

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:58.565
that's telling our fans
that this is for you.

00:11:58.565 --> 00:12:01.560
(eclectic powwow music)

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:05.530
- Has there ever been resistance
from an older generation

00:12:05.530 --> 00:12:08.040
that didn't understand

00:12:10.093 --> 00:12:12.700
the combination of
electronic music with powwow?

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.883
That it's different to them?

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.510
- Yeah, I mean, nobody
has ever really told us

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.010
that we shouldn't be
doing what we're doing,

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.910
but people who don't
necessarily connect with it

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.760
have always said that
they see the relevance

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:29.630
of what we're doing because
it's connecting with youth.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:33.040
So many of us didn't grow up having people

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:35.120
that represented us in
the media, you know.

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.260
We all kinda go back to
the few little things

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.840
that were there, you know,
seeing Buffy Sainte-Marie

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.310
on Sesame Street was a huge one.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:43.690
And I think one of the things

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:45.750
that we have an opportunity to do is start

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:48.725
to give that to a younger generation

00:12:48.725 --> 00:12:53.725
that we are out here being
indigenous on our own terms.

00:12:54.569 --> 00:12:58.526
Not having to be boxed into
something that isn't us.

00:12:58.526 --> 00:13:01.859
(electric powwow music)

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:05.320
- [Bear Witness] We did a
video for our song "Suplex"

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:09.270
about a native wrestler kinda
making it on his own terms.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.130
Next thing we knew after
that video came out

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:14.150
all these parents were sending
in these different photos

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:17.230
of their kids who had made
their own luchador mask,

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:19.790
got them to cut the sleeves
off of their jean jackets

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:22.133
to look like the wrestler in the video.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:26.680
And so, kind of with us
dreaming about what it

00:13:26.680 --> 00:13:30.530
would have been like to have
an indigenous representation

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:33.200
that was true to who we were growing up,

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:36.090
we had given it to a younger generation.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:37.703
Just through our imagining it.

00:13:37.703 --> 00:13:40.550
(electric powwow music)

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.570
- [2oolman] In a lot of ways
that we make a lotta music

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:44.440
that we have to have a purpose for,

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.810
it has to service our
community in some shape or form

00:13:47.810 --> 00:13:49.090
or else it doesn't make sense to do it.

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:52.410
So that's what drives us
to even create, to perform.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.550
It's just, does it make
sense for our community.

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:56.480
That's the thing.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:13:58.264
That's our moral compass that we follow.

00:13:58.264 --> 00:14:01.241
(electric powwow music)

